>>__ Parse.temp_set_grammars $ valOf $ grammarDB {thyname="hol"};
## `FVL_dag`

``` hol4
Term.FVL_dag : term list -> term set -> term set
```

------------------------------------------------------------------------

Computes the free variables of physically shared term graphs.

An invocation `FVL_dag [t1,...,tn] V` returns the same set as
`FVL [t1,...,tn] V`.  It adds the free variables of `t1,...,tn` to the
initial accumulator `V` and preserves variables already in `V`.

During one invocation, an ordinary tree visitor and an intrinsic DAG-summary
visitor advance cooperatively.  The first to complete supplies the exact
result, and both operation-local states are then discarded.  The scheduler
advances the tree visitor at each memo lookup start and before each physical
pointer comparison, so a long linear memo lookup cannot starve it.

The DAG visitor memoizes term nodes by physical identity, preventing repeated
traversal of a shared subterm.  Portable physical-identity lookup still has a
quadratic worst-case cost in the number of distinct nodes.  Both kernels
retain an intrinsic free-variable summary for each visited node.  The standard
kernel additionally retains the free de Bruijn indices of each node; it
interprets only indices referenced by a lazy closure body through
explicit-substitution lookups, without expanding that closure.  Summary
storage can be the product of the numbers of nodes and variables or indices.
Set and closure operations have their usual costs; the cooperative schedule
does not establish a universal linear or wall-time bound.  The initial set and
its comparator are preserved whichever visitor finishes first.

Use `FVL` for ordinary term trees.  Use `FVL_dag` when physical sharing can
make a tree traversal repeat a large subgraph.

### Failure

Never fails on well-formed terms.

### See also

[`Term.FVL`](#Term.FVL), [`Term.empty_varset`](#Term.empty_varset),
